{"kind":"report","text":"CHANGES-REQUESTED at exact clean head 84ec16239144ef679b6aef811ddf2a75bc5002a3, artifact bd298154c8f37edc27872f04c9d3a1b2768cd48b.\n\nBlocker: this head does not meet the request's current-workroom condition and does not unblock the dogfood it claims to unblock. run calls charterIsLive before ClausesFrom. TestTheConnectorRefusesToActWithoutALiveCharter still deliberately rejects a stale charter. The actual charter at sequence 2533 (b86860f2) is stale, and clause 2666 (52b4ec90) is stale because it rests on that charter. Therefore a run naming the current charter still stops before clause admission. TestTheWorkroomsOwnStaleCharterShapeAdmits exercises only ClausesFrom with one stale clause; it omits charterIsLive and cannot demonstrate the stated current-workroom shape. Add an end-to-end command-level test using both stale charter and clause and make the intended policy coherent, or revise the request and repair the durable governance instead.\n\nSecurity: changing stale clauses from fail-closed to admit-with-warning widens the connector's authorization boundary. ClausesFrom does not prove that a clause rests on the --charter selected for this run, so any stale operator clause can be reused beneath a different live charter. The warning is printed after authority has already been granted and requires no operator acknowledgement. Staleness means a signed basis moved; treating that as current authority needs an explicit, consistently enforced application rule, not only a display notice. The safer and simpler path is to keep stale clauses refused, retain the useful named-refusal diagnostics, and state fresh charter/clause acts on stable current bases. If stale admission is intended policy, tie the clause to the checked charter and test basis retirement, stale charter, stale clause, and mismatched charter independently.\n\nArchitecture: affected layers are the Workroom application interpreter/projection and the GitHub connector/CLI surface. The kernel remains semantic-free and the documented kernel/application boundary is preserved; no architecture-page update is required. This does change connector application authorization semantics, so it must be internally consistent across its charter and clause checks before approval.\n\nSimplification: the Reading/Refusal result is a reasonable small way to make rejection diagnosable. The stale-admission branch is not needed to achieve that result. Keeping one fail-closed currentness rule and replacing the malformed durable governance is simpler than two different liveness rules plus a warning channel.\n\nVerification passed: go test ./cmd/gitseq-github ./internal/connector/github; go vet ./cmd/gitseq-github ./internal/connector/github; git diff --check against current main. These gates do not cover the missing end-to-end stale-charter shape.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:bd298154c8f37edc27872f04c9d3a1b2768cd48b","head":"84ec16239144ef679b6aef811ddf2a75bc5002a3","verdict":"changes-requested"}}